---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import Terminal from '../../components/Terminal.astro';
---

<DocsLayout
  title="VFS"
  description="The kernel's observation substrate as a FUSE filesystem. Mount points, namespaces, the three-tier projection lattice, ostk vfs verbs."
  sectionLabel="REFERENCE // OBSERVATION_SUBSTRATE"
>
  <p class="font-headline text-lg text-on-surface-variant italic leading-relaxed max-w-xl mb-12">
    A FUSE projection of the daemon's live registry. <code class="text-primary">ls</code>, <code class="text-primary">cat</code>, <code class="text-primary">tree</code>, and any other Unix tool work on kernel state directly. No JSON parser, no helper command. Source of the projection lattice: <code class="text-primary">VFS_PATH_PROJECTION_LATTICE_RATIFIED</code>.
  </p>

  <!-- 01 — Mount -->
  <section class="mb-16">
    <h2 class="section-label mb-4">01 // MOUNT</h2>
    <p class="font-body text-xs text-secondary mb-3 max-w-lg">
      The VFS is a FUSE filesystem owned by the daemon. The kernel binary must be built with <code class="text-primary">--features fuse</code>; the host needs macFUSE (darwin) or libfuse (linux). On a daemon's first start, <code class="text-primary">auto_mount</code> brings the FUSE filesystem up automatically; explicit verbs cover override and recovery.
    </p>
    <Terminal>
      <pre><span class="text-outline"># Mount at the default location (.ostk/vfs/, blocks until unmount)</span>
<span class="text-outline">$ </span>ostk vfs mount

<span class="text-outline"># Mount at an explicit path</span>
<span class="text-outline">$ </span>ostk vfs mount --mount /tmp/ostk-mount

<span class="text-outline"># Status — declared vs actual mount, owning daemon pid</span>
<span class="text-outline">$ </span>ostk vfs status
declared:
  auto_mount:  true
  mount_point: ~/.ostk/vfs
actual:
  mounted:     true
  owner:       daemon
  owner_pid:   27060
  mount_point: ~/projects/your-repo/.ostk/vfs

<span class="text-outline"># Unmount</span>
<span class="text-outline">$ </span>ostk vfs unmount

<span class="text-outline"># Idempotently reap orphan macfuse entries left by SIGKILL'd daemons</span>
<span class="text-outline">$ </span>ostk vfs unmount-stale</pre>
    </Terminal>
    <p class="font-body text-xs text-outline mt-3 max-w-lg">
      Source: <code class="text-primary">src/kernel/vfs/</code>, <code class="text-primary">src/commands/vfs.rs</code>. The unmount-stale verb (→1663) makes <code class="text-primary">ostk boot</code> safe to re-run after an abnormal kernel exit.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 02 — Namespaces -->
  <section class="mb-16">
    <h2 class="section-label mb-4">02 // NAMESPACES</h2>
    <p class="font-body text-xs text-secondary mb-6 max-w-lg">
      Top-level directories, each a projection of one kernel registry. All paths are relative to the mount root.
    </p>
    <div class="bg-surface-container-low p-6 border-l-2 border-primary max-w-2xl">
      <div class="space-y-2">
        {[
          { ns: 'needles/', desc: 'Open work items keyed by id (needles/1338/title, needles/1338/priority, …). Ratified atoms expose individual fields.' },
          { ns: 'decisions/', desc: 'Decision log entries — keyed (e.g. by stable slug). Each row is one historical decision with reason and timestamp.' },
          { ns: 'fleet/', desc: 'Live agent registry. ls fleet/ enumerates every agent the daemon knows about (active, stale, dead).' },
          { ns: 'proc/', desc: 'Active process table — fleet members currently running, with state under each alias.' },
          { ns: 'journal/', desc: 'Append-only audit log projection. Every kernel-mediated action lands here as a row.' },
          { ns: 'sys/', desc: 'Kernel-internal observability — graph indexer cache (sys/graph/cache/hits, /misses, /entries, /lag/<topic>).' },
          { ns: 'mem/', desc: 'Working memory: hot/warm/cold context pages, pin state.' },
          { ns: 'drivers/', desc: 'Loaded device drivers (fcp-rust, fcp-recall, fcp-screen, fcp-web, …) with their declared capability surfaces.' },
        ].map(n => (
          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-12 gap-2 text-xs font-body py-2 border-b border-surface-variant">
            <code class="text-primary md:col-span-2">{n.ns}</code>
            <span class="text-secondary md:col-span-10">{n.desc}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 03 — Projection lattice -->
  <section class="mb-16">
    <h2 class="section-label mb-4">03 // PROJECTION_LATTICE</h2>
    <p class="font-body text-xs text-secondary mb-6 max-w-lg">
      The same fact is reachable at three resolutions. Reads pick the smallest projection that satisfies the query — atomic when one field is enough, object when the whole row is needed, namespace when enumerating a class. Doctrine: <code class="text-primary">VFS_PATH_PROJECTION_LATTICE_RATIFIED</code>.
    </p>
    <div class="space-y-4 max-w-2xl">
      <div class="bg-surface-container-low p-5 border-l-2 border-primary">
        <div class="font-label text-[10px] text-primary mb-2">ATOM // 1–30 tokens</div>
        <p class="font-body text-xs text-secondary mb-2">A single field. Cheapest read in the system; suitable for hot loops and prompt context.</p>
        <Terminal>
          <pre><span class="text-outline">$ </span>cat .ostk/vfs/needles/1338/title
EPIC: Observation substrate over VFS namespace</pre>
        </Terminal>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-surface-container-low p-5 border-l-2 border-primary">
        <div class="font-label text-[10px] text-primary mb-2">OBJECT // one row</div>
        <p class="font-body text-xs text-secondary mb-2">The full record for one entity — title, priority, status, dependencies, timestamps.</p>
        <Terminal>
          <pre><span class="text-outline">$ </span>cat .ostk/vfs/needles/1338/_object
{`{`}"id":1338,"title":"EPIC: Observation substrate…","priority":"P0","status":"open",…{`}`}</pre>
        </Terminal>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-surface-container-low p-5 border-l-2 border-primary">
        <div class="font-label text-[10px] text-primary mb-2">NAMESPACE // full enumeration</div>
        <p class="font-body text-xs text-secondary mb-2">All members of a class. Pair with <code class="text-primary">grep</code>, <code class="text-primary">find</code>, or <code class="text-primary">xargs</code> for ad-hoc filtering.</p>
        <Terminal>
          <pre><span class="text-outline">$ </span>ls .ostk/vfs/needles/ | wc -l
1413
<span class="text-outline">$ </span>ls .ostk/vfs/fleet/
claude-code-179  ostk-poller-177  ostk-tui-broadcast-160  …</pre>
        </Terminal>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 04 — Why -->
  <section class="mb-16">
    <h2 class="section-label mb-4">04 // WHY_FILESYSTEM</h2>
    <p class="font-body text-xs text-secondary max-w-lg mb-3">
      Every model already knows how to read files. Projecting kernel state as paths means an agent reaches for the registry through the same primitive it uses for source code. No tool call, no JSON parse step, no helper command's output format to learn. The atom tier collapses kernel reads to a few tokens of context — important when context is the working memory.
    </p>
    <p class="font-body text-xs text-outline max-w-lg">
      The VFS is one consumer of the kernel's observation substrate. The audit-row subscriber dispatcher in <code class="text-primary">src/kernel/graph/indexer/</code> feeds the searchable graph index, the VFS projection, and any future read API. Doctrine: <code class="text-primary">LLMOS_OBSERVATION_SUBSTRATE_IS_VFS_NAMESPACE</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 05 — Caveats -->
  <section class="mb-16">
    <h2 class="section-label mb-4">05 // CAVEATS</h2>
    <div class="bg-surface-container-low p-6 border-l-2 border-error max-w-2xl">
      {[
        { issue: 'Build-time feature flag', body: 'Pre-built binaries from os-tack/ostk.ai ship with --features fuse on; building from source requires cargo build --features fuse.' },
        { issue: 'Host FUSE driver', body: 'macOS needs macFUSE (System Settings → Privacy & Security may need approval on first install). Linux needs libfuse3.' },
        { issue: 'Daemon ownership', body: 'The mount belongs to the daemon. Killing the daemon hard (SIGKILL, OOM) leaves a stale macfuse mount entry; ostk vfs unmount-stale clears them.' },
        { issue: 'Read-only by default', body: 'The VFS is a projection — writes go through the normal command surface (ostk work add, ostk decide, etc.), which writes to the journal and the projection follows.' },
        { issue: 'Listing cost on large namespaces', body: 'ls .ostk/vfs/needles/ on a project with thousands of needles enumerates them all. Use globs or scoped subdirectories when possible.' },
      ].map(c => (
        <div class="flex gap-6 text-xs font-body py-3 border-b border-surface-variant">
          <span class="text-on-surface w-56 shrink-0 font-bold">{c.issue}</span>
          <span class="text-secondary">{c.body}</span>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="metadata-ribbon border-t border-surface-variant">
    <span class="font-label text-[10px] text-secondary mr-4">RELATED:</span>
    <a href="/features/vfs/"><Icon name="hub" size="14" class="align-middle mr-1" />VFS_FEATURE</a>
    <a href="/features/audit-trail/"><Icon name="receipt_long" size="14" class="align-middle mr-1" />AUDIT_TRAIL</a>
    <a href="/docs/architecture/"><Icon name="architecture" size="14" class="align-middle mr-1" />ARCHITECTURE</a>
    <a href="/docs/needles/"><Icon name="task" size="14" class="align-middle mr-1" />NEEDLES</a>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
